  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of the Interior obligated $4.7B on contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department obligated 20,638 awards so far, with an average award size of $230K. Obligations fell 27.7% year over year. Fiscal-year history shows a peak in FY24 at $8.1B, followed by $6.6B in FY25 and $4.7B in FY26 to date. Interior continues to buy a mix of construction, engineering, and IT services. Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction is the largest category at $597M, and Computer Systems Design Services and Other Computer Related Services together account for $383M and $379M respectively. The vendor list is led by GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $162M, with AMES FEDERAL CONTRACTING GROUP LLC at $132M and RECORD STEEL AND CONSTRUCTION, INC. at $129M following. This profile highlights where current-year spending is concentrated across industry categories and suppliers.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How have Interior's contract obligations changed year over year?",
        "body": "The Department of the Interior obligated $4.7B in FY2026 to date, down 27.7% year over year. The Department's obligations rose from $5.1B in FY21 to $8.1B in FY24, then declined to $6.6B in FY25 and to $4.7B in FY26 to date. The year-over-year decline reflects lower current-year obligations compared with the prior fiscal year."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are the top procurement categories and vendors for Interior this fiscal year?",
        "body": "Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction is the largest category at $597M, followed by Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $410M and Computer Systems Design Services at $383M. The top vendor is GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $162M, followed by AMES FEDERAL CONTRACTING GROUP LLC at $132M and RECORD STEEL AND CONSTRUCTION, INC. at $129M."
      }
    ]
